# Checkpoint V9D — Current iTantra Communication Stack

This checkpoint explains the current iTantra codebase from **V6A through V9D** in simple terms. The main goal is to describe what is currently implemented and how all the pieces fit together.

---

# 1. V6A — Binary Semantic Message Protocol

V6A defines the actual **meaning of an iTantra message**.

Instead of sending a large JSON object, iTantra converts a speech result into a compact binary message containing information such as:

* message ID
* text
* language
* emotion
* emotion confidence
* voice profile

The protocol also provides default values, so a message can still be represented when emotion information is unavailable.

A message is encoded into a compact binary format before being passed to the transport system.

### In simple terms

I can think of V6A as the **language of the message itself**.

```text
Speech recognized
-> Text
-> Language / message information
-> V6A binary message
```

---

# 2. V6B — Transport Framing and Sequencing

V6B adds a transport frame around the data.

Its job is to identify:

* what type of transport packet this is
* the sequence number
* the payload length
* the actual payload

This lets the receiving side distinguish transport packets and detect ordering/duplicate situations.

The important idea is that the **V6B sequence number belongs to the transport hop**, not to the logical message itself.

```text
V6A message
-> V6B transport frame
-> sequence number
-> payload
```

---

# 3. V7 — Fragmentation and Reassembly

Bluetooth packets have limited space, so a large V6A message cannot always fit inside one transport packet.

V7 solves this by splitting a large payload into multiple fragments.

Each fragment belongs to the same fragment group and contains enough information for the receiver to rebuild the original message.

Fragments can arrive out of order and are reassembled when the complete set is available.

### In simple terms

```text
Large V6A message
-> split into fragments
-> fragment 1
-> fragment 2
-> fragment 3
-> ...
-> receiver collects fragments
-> original V6A message
```

Small messages can continue without fragmentation.

---

# 4. V8 — BLE Transport Reliability

V8 is responsible for making the **individual Bluetooth link reliable**.

It provides:

* ACK
* NACK
* retransmission
* retry limits
* timeout handling
* transport sequence handling
* duplicate handling

The important concept is that V8 reliability is **per BLE connection**.

For example:

```text
Phone A
-> Bluetooth link
-> Phone B
```

has its own transport reliability.

In a mesh:

```text
A -> B
B -> C
C -> D
```

each Bluetooth hop is treated as its own transport link.

```text
A <-> B
-> V8 reliability

B <-> C
-> V8 reliability

C <-> D
-> V8 reliability
```

---

# 5. V8 and the Voice Pipeline

The existing voice system connects speech recognition to the transport system.

The basic flow is:

```text
Microphone
-> local STT
-> recognized text
-> V6A message
-> transport
```

At the receiving side:

```text
Received message
-> decode
-> text
-> TTS
-> speaker
```

The system also handles the voice feedback problem so that received speech is not immediately re-captured by the microphone while it is being played.

This allows iTantra to behave more like a **push-to-talk voice communication system**, while transmitting text rather than raw audio.

```text
Phone A speaks
-> STT
-> text message
-> Bluetooth
-> Phone B
-> TTS
-> Phone B hears speech
```

---

# 6. Why the system sends text instead of raw audio

The important design idea is that iTantra does not need to transmit continuous speech audio across the network.

Instead:

```text
Voice
-> STT
-> Text
-> Network
-> Text
-> TTS
-> Voice
```

This makes the transmitted information much smaller than sending the original audio stream.

The network therefore transports the **meaning of the speech**, while speech generation happens at the receiving device.

---

# 7. BITCHAT — Network Layer

V9 introduced a separate networking layer called **BITCHAT**.

BITCHAT sits above the existing BLE transport.

Its job is no longer simply:

> “Send this packet to my directly connected Bluetooth device.”

Instead, it answers:

> “How can this packet travel through a network of participating phones?”

The BITCHAT layer contains concepts such as:

* node identity
* packet identity
* destination identity
* TTL
* peer information
* deduplication
* relay behavior
* flooding
* mesh discovery

```text
Application message
-> BITCHAT network
-> one or more BLE hops
-> destination
```

---

# 8. BITCHAT Node ID

Every iTantra mesh node has a BITCHAT **Node ID**.

This identifies the device within the mesh.

The Node ID is different from the Bluetooth transport sequence number.

```text
Node ID
-> identifies the phone/node

V6B sequence
-> identifies a transport packet on a BLE hop
```

The Node ID is used for mesh-level identity.

---

# 9. BITCHAT Packet Identity

Every BITCHAT packet has:

* source Node ID
* destination Node ID
* packet ID
* TTL
* packet type
* payload

The important thing is that the BITCHAT identity stays the same while a packet travels through the mesh.

For example:

```text
A -> B -> C -> D -> F
```

The BITCHAT packet can continue to represent:

```text
source = A
destination = F
packet = X
```

at every hop.

---

# 10. BITCHAT Destination ID

V9C added a destination Node ID to the BITCHAT packet.

This allows the network to distinguish between:

```text
"This packet is for me"
```

and:


"This packet is passing through me"
```

For a unicast message:


Source = A
Destination = F
```

the path can look like:


A
-> B
-> C
-> D
-> F
```

B, C and D forward the packet.

F is the node that delivers the message to the local voice application.

```text
A -> B -> C -> D -> F
              relay -> destination
```

---

# 11. TTL — Controlling How Far Packets Travel

BITCHAT packets have a TTL value.

Each relay reduces the TTL.

This prevents packets from continuing through the network indefinitely.

For example:

```text
A
-> B   TTL decreases
-> C   TTL decreases
-> D   TTL decreases
-> F
```

When TTL reaches its limit, the packet is no longer forwarded.

```text
TTL
-> limits packet lifetime
-> prevents endless forwarding
```

---

# 12. Deduplication

In a mesh, the same packet can reach a phone through multiple paths.

For example:

```text
        B
       / \
      /   \
A -> C     D -> F
      \   /
       \ /
        E
```

The same BITCHAT packet may arrive through different paths.

The packet identity lets the node recognize:

> “I have already seen this packet.”

The duplicate is then ignored instead of being forwarded again.


Packet X arrives
-> not seen
-> process
-> forward

Packet X arrives again
-> already seen
-> drop duplicate
```

This prevents flooding from becoming an endless loop.

---

# 13. Peer Registry

BITCHAT maintains information about the phones that are **directly connected through BLE**.

A direct peer means:


My phone
<-> Bluetooth connection
<-> another phone
```

The peer registry tracks the state of these direct relationships.

The states include:


DISCOVERED
-> CONNECTED
-> STALE
```

This is about **direct BLE neighbors**, not every phone somewhere in the mesh.

---

# 14. ANNOUNCE — Direct Neighbor Identity

ANNOUNCE is used to identify a **direct BLE neighbor**.

For example:


A <-> B
```

A sends an ANNOUNCE saying:

```text
"I am Node A."
```

B then knows:

```text
This BLE connection corresponds to Node A.
```

Similarly, B can announce itself to A.

ANNOUNCE is intentionally **not a mesh-flooded message**.

```text
A <-> B
ANNOUNCE
-> direct relationship only
```

It is not:

```text
A -> B -> C
ANNOUNCE
```

because C must not mistakenly believe that the BLE connection to B belongs to A.

---

# 15. Mesh Relay

V9B introduced the actual BITCHAT relay engine.

A node can receive a packet and forward it to other connected peers.

The general behavior is:

```text
Receive
-> validate
-> check duplicate
-> process locally when appropriate
-> reduce TTL
-> forward to eligible peers
```

The sender that delivered the packet can be excluded from the immediate forwarding decision to avoid unnecessary back-and-forth.

This gives us a controlled flooding mesh rather than a fixed route.

```text
A
-> B
-> C
-> D
-> F
```

The network does not need to maintain one permanent path for the packet.

---

# 16. V9C — BLE and BITCHAT Integration

V9C connected the BITCHAT network layer to the existing BLE/V8 transport.

The architecture is now:

```text
BITCHAT packet
-> V6B transport frame
-> BLE/V8
-> another phone
```

When a BITCHAT packet is forwarded, the BITCHAT identity stays the same, while the V6B transport sequence is appropriate for the new BLE hop.

Therefore:

```text
BITCHAT identity
-> remains the same across mesh

V6B sequence
-> changes for each transport hop
```

---

# 17. Per-Hop Transport in the Mesh

The transport hierarchy is therefore:

```text
A
-> BLE/V8
-> B
-> BLE/V8
-> C
-> BLE/V8
-> D
-> BLE/V8
-> F
```

while the network packet remains:

```text
source = A
destination = F
packet = X
```

This keeps the **mesh network layer** separate from the **BLE transport layer**.

---

# 18. Relay vs Destination

The destination field makes it possible to distinguish:

```text
Relay node
-> forward packet
-> do not deliver it to the voice application
```

from:

```text
Destination node
-> recognize its own Node ID
-> deliver packet
-> decode message
-> TTS
```

For:

```text
A -> B -> C -> F
```

the intended behavior is:

```text
A
-> send

B
-> relay

C
-> relay

F
-> receive
-> decode
-> TTS
```

---

# 19. V9D — Mesh-Wide Node Discovery

V9D adds a separate **mesh discovery mechanism**.

ANNOUNCE tells a phone:

> “This directly connected BLE peer is Node X.”

DISCOVERY tells the mesh:

> “Node X exists somewhere in the mesh.”

These are deliberately different.


ANNOUNCE
-> direct neighbor identity

DISCOVERY
-> mesh-wide node discovery
```

---

# 20. DISCOVERY Packets

A DISCOVERY packet contains the identity of the node being advertised.

The discovery information is small:

```text
advertised Node ID
-> discovery version
```

The packet itself travels through the BITCHAT mesh.

Therefore:

```text
F
-> DISCOVERY
-> B
-> C
-> A
```

A can eventually learn:

```text
Node F exists
```

even when F is not directly connected to A.

---

# 21. Mesh Discovery Registry

The discovered Node IDs are stored separately from direct BLE peers.

This gives us two different types of information:

### Direct peer information

```text
BLE connection
-> directly connected Node ID
```

### Mesh discovery information

```text
Node ID
-> known to exist somewhere in the mesh
```

A remotely discovered node is **not treated as a direct Bluetooth peer**.

```text
A discovers F
-> A knows F exists
-> F is NOT considered A's direct BLE peer
```

---

# 22. Discovery Expiration

Discovery information is temporary.

A discovered node is stored for a limited period and can be refreshed when rediscovered.

The registry is also bounded so it cannot grow indefinitely.

In simple terms:

```text
Node discovered
-> stored

Node discovered again
-> refresh information

Node not seen for long enough
-> expires
```

---

# 23. Discovery vs Routing

The current mesh knows:

```text
"Node F exists."
```

It does **not** maintain a permanent route such as:


F -> D -> C -> B


That distinction is important.

The current architecture uses:

```text
Node discovery
+
destination identity
+
controlled flooding
```

rather than a traditional routing table.

---

# 24. Advisory Hop Count

The discovery registry may store an approximate hop count as metadata.

For example:

```text
Node F
-> hopCount = 2
```

This is only advisory information.

It is **not** used as:

```text
routing information
destination selection
relay selection
delivery logic
```

So:

```text
hopCount
-> informational metadata only
```

---

# 25. Current End-to-End Concept

Putting all the implemented parts together, the current communication system works conceptually like this:

```text
Phone A
-> iTantra STT
-> V6A semantic message
-> V7 fragmentation when required
-> BITCHAT packet
-> destination Node ID
-> V6B transport frame
-> V8 BLE transport
-> Bluetooth
-> Phone B
-> BITCHAT relay
-> V6B/V8
-> Bluetooth
-> Phone C
-> BITCHAT relay
-> V6B/V8
-> Bluetooth
-> ...
-> Destination Phone F
-> BITCHAT destination check
-> V7 reassembly when required
-> V6A decoding
-> text
-> iTantra TTS
-> speaker
```

---

# Current iTantra System

The complete system currently implemented can be visualized as:

```text
PHONE A
-> iTantra Software
-> Microphone
-> Local STT
-> V6A Semantic Message
-> V7 Fragmentation if required
-> BITCHAT Network Packet
-> Source Node ID
-> Destination Node ID
-> Packet ID + TTL + Deduplication
-> V6B Transport Frame
-> V8 BLE Reliability
-> Bluetooth LE
-> PHONE B
-> iTantra BITCHAT Relay
-> V6B / V8
-> Bluetooth LE
-> PHONE C
-> iTantra BITCHAT Relay
-> V6B / V8
-> Bluetooth LE
-> ... any participating relay phones ...
-> Bluetooth LE
-> DESTINATION PHONE F
-> iTantra BITCHAT Destination
-> V7 Reassembly if required
-> V6A Semantic Message
-> Text
-> Local TTS
-> Speaker
```

### In one simple picture


        iTantra Mesh over Bluetooth LE

PHONE A
-> iTantra
-> STT
-> V6A
-> V7
-> BITCHAT
-> V6B
-> V8
-> Bluetooth
        ↓
PHONE B
-> BITCHAT Relay
-> V6B
-> V8
-> Bluetooth
        ↓
PHONE C
-> BITCHAT Relay
-> V6B
-> V8
-> Bluetooth
        ↓
      ........
        ↓
PHONE F
-> BITCHAT Destination
-> V7
-> V6A
-> TTS
-> Speaker

